 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572013 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572013 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572013 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186571909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572013 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "471634b7-feb0-50b9-9eda-91e81c3b2ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186571786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186571626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572021 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5428c292-72be-5b0f-bf8c-5686e732abae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572134 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/31/314cd1d2e150c078fa7232c81125de44fb8c26c176d2a1f32ab02fbae2eef299.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $m=-g$\n\nII. $a=z$\n\nIII. $g=-\\frac{1}{2}m$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52f19af3-7870-568c-aad1-0f002a1758f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572134 #edb/ref 1074 "- Statement I is false. The magnitude of $m$ is greater than the magnitude of $g.$ So, $m\\ne -g.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $a$ and $z$ have opposite directions. So, $a\\ne z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, vectors $g$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also the magnitude of $g$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $m.$ So, $g=-\\frac{1}{2}m.$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572153 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b2c3f59df41cb406ddfc41eea81a979dec578ba990032f0ff4f2f9792f58c8b2.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ and $2a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9199d3db-0aa2-51c3-b858-22576fdfaa53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572153 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that both $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ and $2a$ must be parallel to $a.$\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ is $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is negative.\n\n![](images/13/13e4de227344b9eb7d1867cca0d05d9bcb647662a0c732398b42277ac379d361.png)\n\n \n\nNext, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is twice the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is the following.\n\n![](images/13/13ca18bebfdb3c738d0a1198d0ce4235bfd77185bcf232f79f9ba089ce3b6d38.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186572021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572158 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/605c5def8bc643f0f601f89b9f8d7b556ac94206867dbe8904ef746c83f02fc0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a.$ Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $\\frac{3}{2}a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99744ab1-80a8-57ad-804e-15c6f48c9bd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572158 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a.$\nNow, we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ of the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{3}{2}$ is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is the following.\n\n![](images/59/591682cfbe0f50347d88f894f4c2b740fca140e8a93838f5c53022f9f50ee6da.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186572000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572160 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b)$ and $4\\sqrt{7}\\,a+7b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9cb3a4aa-4089-5a0f-987c-888c6fdaeaf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572160 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt{7}\\,a+7b=\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{7}(4a+\\sqrt{7}b)=\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(4a+\\sqrt{7}b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=4a+\\sqrt{7}b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{7}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\sqrt{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20395" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572164 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\lambda (12a-52b)$ and $-3a+13b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4c754c3-d143-5941-8c38-1a8c75e6be25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572164 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-3a+13b=\\lambda (12a-52b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}(12a-52b)=\\lambda (12a-52b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(12a-52b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=12a-52b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186572013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572198 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/92/9236e136f26433023d806181fcfbef9c05d4976f75e14f74840ae7f48a35da99.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $r=3q$\n\nII. $s=2t$\n\nIII. $p=-s$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f081ecc9-be21-500f-81d8-5fe5809e98ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572198 #edb/ref 1074 "- Statement I is false. The vectors $r$ and $q$ have the opposite directions. So, $r\\ne 3q.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $s$ and $t$ are parallel and have the same directions. Also, the magnitude of $s$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $t.$ So, $s=2t.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $p$ and $s$ are parallel and have the opposite directions. Also, their magnitudes are the same. So, $p=-s.$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, and statement I is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
